// Expected: e0e0e0e0e0f0 at one session, e1e1e1e1e1f0 at two.
//
// A five-way SPLIT of a CONCAT bundle, checked twice on the way in. `k` is
// shared and never leaks, so the attacker cannot produce any (encrypted, tag)
// pair other than the honest one: all it can do is corrupt the ciphertext and
// fail Bob's ASSERT. Bob then computes nothing at all — bundle_b and a_b..e_b
// never come into existence — so there is no value for a..e to disagree with.
// An abort is not a divergence, and all five equivalence queries hold.
//
// These read e1e1e1e1e1 until `query_equivalence` learned to refuse a state
// whose slots had been truncated at the halt: the dropped a_b..e_b resolved to
// bare constants, which compared unequal to a..e. See equivalence_halt_scope.vp.
//
// Under `--sessions 2` all five fail again, and this time for real: `k` is
// long-term, so a second session's (encrypted, tag) verifies perfectly for the
// first session's Bob. He accepts that bundle, both his checks pass, and he
// ends up holding a#2..e#2 while Alice holds a..e. Nothing in the ciphertext
// binds it to a session. The contrast between the two codes is the point —
// halting Bob was never a divergence, but feeding him another session's
// bundle is one.

attacker[active]

principal Alice[
	knows private k
	generates a, b, c, d, e
	bundle = CONCAT(a, b, c, d, e)
	encrypted = ENC(k, bundle)
	tag = MAC(k, encrypted)
]

Alice -> Bob: encrypted, tag

principal Bob[
	knows private k
	_ = ASSERT(MAC(k, encrypted), tag)?
	bundle_b = DEC(k, encrypted)
	a_b, b_b, c_b, d_b, e_b = SPLIT(bundle_b)?
]

queries[
	equivalence? a, a_b
	equivalence? b, b_b
	equivalence? c, c_b
	equivalence? d, d_b
	equivalence? e, e_b
	freshness? a
]
